# This file documents the SynapseOS boot sequence.
# Actual unit files are copied from each component's systemd/ directory
# by build.sh into airootfs/etc/systemd/system/.
#
# Boot order:
#
#  basic.target
#     └─ synapse_kmod (modprobe via modules-load.d)
#           └─ synapd.service          ← AI daemon (must come first)
#                 └─ synguard.service  ← security monitor
#                       └─ display-manager.service
#                             └─ synui (via graphical-session.target)
#
# The AI daemon starts before the display manager so the compositor
# can connect to it immediately on first frame.
#
# Since 0.2.8 the ISO ships NO model (build.sh --with-model embeds one), so
# synapd starts here with nothing to load and runs in shell-assist mode. That
# is expected on the live media — `syn status` saying "model ✗ not installed"
# is not a fault to chase. syn-install asks which model to download onto the
# target.
#
# DO NOT re-add var-lib-synapd-models.mount, which came from 0.1.0 and mounted
# a 6G tmpfs over /var/lib/synapd/models. Once the ISO started shipping a real
# synapse.gguf at exactly that path, the tmpfs did nothing but HIDE it: on the
# live media every AI feature was dead while the ISO still paid the 4.3GB.
# Installed systems were unaffected only because syn-install read around the
# mount, from /run/archiso/airootfs/var/lib/synapd/models/*.gguf — which is why
# it looked like "the installed system works but the live one doesn't". A
# --with-model build would walk straight back into that, and it buys nothing
# either way: the live root is an overlay (squashfs lower + tmpfs upper), so
# that directory is already writable in RAM if anything wants to drop a model
# in it.
